@section kfc_intro Introduction
This document describes briefly the AUFIT program. AUFIT is the acronym for Atari 
Universal Floppy-Disk Image Tool. 

The program reads several Floppy Disk image's formats used on the Atari platform.
Once read the FD image is analyzed and its content is displayed using several 
graphical charts. It is possible to deeply analyzed and display the content of 
any track or sectors as they would be read using the WD1772 FDC read track, 
read address, and read sector commands. It is also possible to display the 
Disk Sector Layout and Disk Data Layout. These two graphical outputs allow 
to see how the sectors and the data are layout on the diskette. The program 
also tests for most (if not all) FD protection mechanisms used on the Atari platform.
Currently the program supports the images generated by SuperCard Pro and KryoFlux
devices (flux transition level).

One of the most interesting feature for Atari users is the capability to
output the content of the diskette in the Pasti STX format. This format can
be read by Steem and Saint emulators (and soon by Hatari).
Therefore with Aufit you do not need to wait for conversion from manufacturer.
As soon as you have finished to create the image of your disk you can generate
the stx file to use in your favorite emulator.

Usage has been designed to be as simple as possible for casual users. However 
the program also provides powerful features for advanced users.

@section _proguse Program Usage

Currently the program does not requires to be installed and is provided directly 
as an executable: Aufit.exe. To run the program just double click the executable.

The program run under Windows Vista SP2, Windows 7 SP1, and Widows 8 or above. 
The program requires that the Microsoft.NET Framework 4.5 or above is installed. 
It also requires the Ookii Dialogs class library. The Ookii DLL is provided along 
with the executable and documentation.

Basic usage consist in reading a complete Floppy Disk image by either specifying a 
directory of stream file (from KryoFLux) or a .scp file (from SuperCard Pro).
Then you can click the Write Disk button to directly write a .stx file.
The only condition for this to work is to use good quality images as explained
in detail in the documentation.

For more information please refer to Aufit User's Manual.
Enjoy

@author	Jean Louis-Guerin

